Here the grid stays unchanged, so a separate `visited` matrix keeps track of cells already explored. I use an explicit stack for DFS. The first unvisited land cell in a connected group starts exactly one traversal.

This costs more memory than changing `1` to `0`, but it is useful when the original grid must be preserved.

For an all-water grid, no stack is created and the method returns 0. Diagonal cells are not connected because only four directions are checked.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.length == 0 || grid[0].length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = grid.length;
        int cols = grid[0].length;
        boolean[][] visited = new boolean[rows][cols];
        int islands = 0;
        int[][] directions = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                if (grid[row][col] != '1' || visited[row][col]) {
                    continue;
                }

                islands++;
                java.util.ArrayDeque<int[]> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
                stack.push(new int[]{row, col});
                visited[row][col] = true;

                while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
                    int[] cell = stack.pop();

                    for (int[] direction : directions) {
                        int nextRow = cell[0] + direction[0];
                        int nextCol = cell[1] + direction[1];

                        if (nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < rows &&
                            nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < cols &&
                            !visited[nextRow][nextCol] &&
                            grid[nextRow][nextCol] == '1') {
                            visited[nextRow][nextCol] = true;
                            stack.push(new int[]{nextRow, nextCol});
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return islands;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols)`.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the visited matrix and stack. The original grid is not modified.

I used a queue here instead of recursion. When the scan finds land, the island count goes up once, and the queue visits all horizontally or vertically connected land cells.

Cells are marked as `0` when they enter the queue. This is important because it stops one cell from being added more than once. The input grid is modified.

Dry run for `[[1,1,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]]`: the first queue removes the connected top-left group, so the count becomes 1. The last `1` starts another queue, giving 2 islands.

```java
class Solution {
    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.length == 0 || grid[0].length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = grid.length;
        int cols = grid[0].length;
        int islands = 0;
        int[][] directions = {{-1, 0}, {1, 0}, {0, -1}, {0, 1}};

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                if (grid[row][col] != '1') {
                    continue;
                }

                islands++;
                java.util.ArrayDeque<int[]> queue = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
                queue.offer(new int[]{row, col});
                grid[row][col] = '0';

                while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
                    int[] cell = queue.poll();

                    for (int[] direction : directions) {
                        int nextRow = cell[0] + direction[0];
                        int nextCol = cell[1] + direction[1];

                        if (nextRow >= 0 && nextRow < rows &&
                            nextCol >= 0 && nextCol < cols &&
                            grid[nextRow][nextCol] == '1') {
                            grid[nextRow][nextCol] = '0';
                            queue.offer(new int[]{nextRow, nextCol});
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return islands;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols)`, since each cell is processed at most once.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the queue in the largest island.

This version treats each land cell as a separate group first. Only the right and down neighbors need to be checked, because checking all four directions would repeat the same union work.

Every successful union joins two islands and reduces the current count. Water cells are never added to the set structure. The grid itself is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution{
    vector<int> parent;
    vector<int> rankValue;

    int findRoot(int node) {
        if (parent[node] == node) {
            return node;
        }
        parent[node] = findRoot(parent[node]);
        return parent[node];
    }

    bool unite(int first, int second) {
        int rootFirst = findRoot(first);
        int rootSecond = findRoot(second);

        if (rootFirst == rootSecond) {
            return false;
        }

        if (rankValue[rootFirst] < rankValue[rootSecond]) {
            swap(rootFirst, rootSecond);
        }

        parent[rootSecond] = rootFirst;
        if (rankValue[rootFirst] == rankValue[rootSecond]) {
            rankValue[rootFirst]++;
        }
        return true;
    }

public:
    int numIslands(vector<vector<char>> &grid){
        if (grid.empty() || grid[0].empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = grid.size();
        int cols = grid[0].size();
        int totalCells = rows * cols;
        parent.resize(totalCells);
        rankValue.assign(totalCells, 0);

        for (int id = 0; id < totalCells; id++) {
            parent[id] = id;
        }

        int islands = 0;
        int rowStep[2] = {1, 0};
        int colStep[2] = {0, 1};

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                if (grid[row][col] != '1') {
                    continue;
                }

                islands++;
                int currentId = row * cols + col;

                for (int direction = 0; direction < 2; direction++) {
                    int nextRow = row + rowStep[direction];
                    int nextCol = col + colStep[direction];

                    if (nextRow < rows && nextCol < cols &&
                        grid[nextRow][nextCol] == '1') {
                        int nextId = nextRow * cols + nextCol;
                        if (unite(currentId, nextId)) {
                            islands--;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return islands;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols * alpha(rows * cols))`, where `alpha` grows very slowly.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the parent and rank arrays.

A land cell starts one island. I count it, then recursively visit its four neighbors and turn every reached land cell into water. That prevents the same island from being counted again.

The grid is changed in place. An empty grid is handled before reading its column count.

```python
class Solution:
    def numIslands(self, grid):
        if not grid or not grid[0]:
            return 0

        rows = len(grid)
        cols = len(grid[0])
        islands = 0

        def flood_fill(row, col):
            if row < 0 or row >= rows or col < 0 or col >= cols:
                return
            if grid[row][col] != '1':
                return

            grid[row][col] = '0'
            flood_fill(row - 1, col)
            flood_fill(row + 1, col)
            flood_fill(row, col - 1)
            flood_fill(row, col + 1)

        for row in range(rows):
            for col in range(cols):
                if grid[row][col] == '1':
                    islands += 1
                    flood_fill(row, col)

        return islands
```

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols)`.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` in the worst case for the recursive call stack.

